<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-saddle-example">
<title>Saddle Point</title>

<definition xml:id="def-skel-saddle-point">
<title>Saddle Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
A differentiable function <m>f(x,y)</m> has a <term>saddle point</term>
at a critical point <m>(a,b)</m> if in every open disk centered at
<m>(a,b)</m> there are domain points <m>(x,y)</m> where
<m>f(a,b) \lt f(x,y)</m> and domain points <m>(x,y)</m> where
<m>f(a,b) \gt f(x,y)</m>. The corresponding point
<m>(a,b,f(a,b))</m> on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> is called a
saddle point of the surface.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-saddle-point">
<title>Saddle Point</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function
<md xml:id="eq-skel-saddle-function" number="yes">
f(x,y) = \frac{y^2}{3} - \frac{x^2}{3}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Show that <m>f</m> has a saddle point at <m>(0,0)</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(This is the hyperbolic paraboloid of
<xref ref="subsec-skel-hyperbolic-paraboloid"/>. Find the partials and
confirm that <m>(0,0)</m> is the only critical point. Then, to match
<xref ref="def-skel-saddle-point"/>, you need points of <em>both</em>
kinds in every disk around the origin: restrict <m>f</m> to each
coordinate axis in turn and look at the sign. Note at the end that both
partials vanish here and yet there is no extremum.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$f_x =$ \qquad $f_y =$ \qquad critical point:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {Along the $x$-axis: $f(x,0) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {Along the $y$-axis: $f(0,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Conclusion:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the partial derivatives and critical point, for the function along each coordinate axis, and for the conclusion.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-saddle-video">
<caption>
The hyperbolic paraboloid <m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m>. Along the
<m>x</m>-axis the surface falls below the origin, and along the
<m>y</m>-axis it rises above the origin, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle
point.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-saddle-point"
       source="videos/saddle_point_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/saddle_point_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-saddle-traces">
<caption>
The traces of <m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m> along the two coordinate planes.
In the plane <m>y = 0</m> the trace <m>z = -x^2/3</m> opens downward,
while in the plane <m>x = 0</m> the trace <m>z = y^2/3</m> opens
upward, so <m>(0,0)</m> is a saddle point.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>Two parabolas through the origin, one opening upward and one opening downward.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Two parabolas through the origin showing the traces of the hyperbolic paraboloid z equals y squared over 3 minus x squared over 3, drawn on one pair of axes: the horizontal axis stands for x or y and the vertical axis is z. The blue parabola opens upward and is the trace along the y axis. The red parabola opens downward and is the trace along the x axis. The origin, where they meet, is the saddle point.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-saddle-traces"><![CDATA[
// The traces of z = y^2/3 - x^2/3 in the two coordinate planes, drawn on one pair of
// axes: z = y^2/3 (plane x = 0) opens upward, z = -x^2/3 (plane y = 0) opens downward.
import graph;
unitsize(1.7cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-2.4, 2.4, -1.7, 1.7, "$x$ or $y$", "$z$");

real up(real t) { return t^2/3; }
real down(real t) { return -t^2/3; }
draw(graph(up, -2.1, 2.1), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
draw(graph(down, -2.1, 2.1), red + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$\displaystyle z=\frac{y^2}{3}$", (1.45, up(1.45)), NW, blue);
label("$\displaystyle z=-\frac{x^2}{3}$", (1.45, down(1.45)), SW, red);

dot((0, 0), black + linewidth(5pt));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-second-derivative-test">
<title>The Second Derivative Test</title>

<theorem xml:id="thm-skel-second-derivative-test">
<title>Theorem II (The Second Derivative Test)</title>
<statement>
<p>
Suppose that <m>f(x,y)</m> and its first and second partial derivatives
are continuous throughout a disk centered at <m>(a,b)</m> and that
<m>f_x(a,b) = f_y(a,b) = 0</m>. Then
</p>

<p>
<ol marker="i.">
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>local maximum</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx} \lt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \gt 0</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>local minimum</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx} \gt 0</m> and <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \gt 0</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>f</m> has a <term>saddle point</term> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 \lt 0</m> at <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<em>The test is inconclusive</em> at <m>(a,b)</m> if
<m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = 0</m> at <m>(a,b)</m>. In this case, we
must find some other way to determine the behavior of <m>f</m> at
<m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
The expression <m>f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</m> is called the
<term>discriminant</term> or the <term>Hessian</term> of <m>f(x,y)</m>,
and it is easiest to remember as a determinant:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-hessian" number="yes">
H(x,y) =
\begin{vmatrix}
f_{xx} \amp f_{xy} \\
f_{xy} \amp f_{yy}
\end{vmatrix}
= f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2.
</md>
</p>

<p>
Before using it, get the shape of the test clear. (Which quantity is
consulted <em>first</em>, and what does its sign decide? Only in one of
those branches does a second quantity get consulted <mdash/> which, and
what does it decide there? Note also which entry of
<xref ref="eq-skel-hessian"/> is used twice, and why
<xref ref="thm-skel-mixed-derivative"/> permits that.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {First look at: \qquad\qquad which decides:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {Then, if needed, look at: \qquad which decides:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {Why $f_{xy}$ may be used for both off-diagonal entries:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the order in which the two quantities of the test are consulted and for why the mixed partial appears twice.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-quartic-extrema">
<title>Finding Local Extrema and Saddle Points</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the local maximum and minimum values and saddle points of the
function
<md xml:id="eq-skel-quartic-function" number="yes">
z = f(x,y) = x^4 + y^4 - 4xy.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Set both first partials to zero. The resulting pair is symmetric in
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>; substitute one into the other to get a single
equation in one variable, and factor it completely <mdash/> only the real
roots count. Then form <xref ref="eq-skel-hessian"/> once, as a formula in
<m>x</m> and <m>y</m>, and evaluate it at each critical point rather than
recomputing. Mark the critical points on the grid, which is the
intersection of the two curves you solved.)
</p>

<sidebyside widths="52% 44%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (8,15);
\draw[gray!35] (0,12) -- (8,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (8,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (8,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,14.7) {$f_x =$ \qquad $f_y =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,11.7) {Solving:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.7) {$H(x,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,4.2) {Classification of each point:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the first partial derivatives, for solving the system, for the Hessian, and for the classification of each critical point.</shortdescription>
</image>
<image>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-1.6,-1.6) grid[step=0.25] (1.6,1.6);
\draw[->, thick] (-1.6,0) -- (1.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1.6) -- (0,1.9) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-1,1} \draw (\x,-0.05) -- (\x,0.05) node[below=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,1} \draw (-0.05,\y) -- (0.05,\y) node[left=3pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
<shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid from negative one and a half to one and a half in both directions, for sketching the two curves and marking their intersections.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-quartic-video">
