\draw[gray!35] (0,6.5) -- (15,6.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.7) {$d^2 = f(x,y) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.2) {Why minimizing $d^2$ is enough:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.7) {Critical point:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.2) {$H =$ \qquad so the point is a:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$d =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in five rows, for the squared distance as a function of two variables, for why squaring is harmless, for the critical point, for the Hessian test, and for the distance.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Maximum and Absolute Minimum</title>

<p>
In the next example, we will see how to find absolute extrema of a
function <m>f(x,y)</m> over a closed bounded region <m>R</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-absolute-extrema">
<title>Absolute Extrema over a Rectangle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function
<m>f(x,y) = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> on the rectangle
<md xml:id="eq-skel-rectangle-region" number="yes">
R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}.
</md>
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(Three steps. First the critical points of <m>f</m> lying <em>inside</em>
<m>R</m>. Then the boundary, which is not one curve but four edges: on
each, one variable is fixed, so <m>f</m> becomes a one-variable function
on a closed interval <mdash/> handle each with single-variable calculus,
and do not forget the endpoints. Finally compare every value collected.
Note that no second derivative test is needed anywhere here; say why.
Label the four edges on <xref ref="fig-skel-rectangle-region"/> as you
go.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,20);
\draw[gray!35] (0,17) -- (15,17);
\draw[gray!35] (0,13.5) -- (15,13.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7.5) -- (15,7.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,19.7) {I. Interior critical points:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,16.7) {II. On $L_1$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.2) {\phantom{II. }On $L_2$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {\phantom{II. }On $L_3$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.2) {\phantom{II. }On $L_4$: $f =$ \qquad min: \qquad max:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {III. Absolute max: \qquad\qquad absolute min:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in six rows, for the interior critical points, for the function restricted to each of the four edges with its extreme values, and for the absolute extrema.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-rectangle-region-video">
<caption>
The closed bounded rectangle
<m>R = \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 3,\; 0 \le y \le 2\}</m> with its four
edges <m>L_1</m>, <m>L_2</m>, <m>L_3</m>, and <m>L_4</m>, and the
critical point <m>(1,1)</m> in its interior.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-rectangle-region"
       source="videos/rectangle_region_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/rectangle_region_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-rectangle-region">
<caption>
The rectangle <m>R</m> and its four edges. To find the absolute
extrema, we evaluate <m>f</m> at the critical points inside <m>R</m>
and compare with the extreme values of <m>f</m> along each edge.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>A rectangle with its four edges labelled and one point marked inside it.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
The rectangle R in the x y plane, with corners at the origin, 3, 0, 3, 2, and 0, 2, shaded gray. Its four edges are labeled L 1 along the bottom, L 2 along the right, L 3 along the top, and L 4 along the left. The critical point 1, 1 is marked inside the rectangle.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-rectangle-region"><![CDATA[
// The rectangle R = [0,3] x [0,2] with its four edges L1, L2, L3, L4 and the critical
// point (1,1) in its interior.
import graph;
unitsize(1.9cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

fill(box((0, 0), (3, 2)), gray(0.87));
axes(-0.6, 3.9, -0.6, 2.8, "$x$", "$y$");

pen c1 = blue, c2 = red, c3 = rgb(0, 0.5, 0), c4 = rgb(0.9, 0.5, 0);
draw((0, 0)--(3, 0), c1 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((3, 0)--(3, 2), c2 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((0, 2)--(3, 2), c3 + linewidth(2.2pt));
draw((0, 0)--(0, 2), c4 + linewidth(2.2pt));
label("$L_1$", (1.5, 0), S, c1);
label("$L_2$", (3, 1), E, c2);
label("$L_3$", (1.5, 2), N, c3);
label("$L_4$", (0, 1), W, c4);
label("$3$", (3, 0), S);
label("$2$", (0, 2), W);

label("$R$", (2.2, 1.3));
dot((1, 1), rgb(0.5, 0, 0) + linewidth(5.5pt));
label("$(1,1)$", (1, 1), NE, rgb(0.5, 0, 0));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-absolute-extrema-surface-video">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = x^2 - 2xy + 2y</m> over the rectangle <m>R</m>. The
absolute maximum <m>f(3,0) = 9</m> and the absolute minimum
<m>f(0,0) = f(2,2) = 0</m> occur on the boundary of <m>R</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-absolute-extrema-surface"
       source="videos/absolute_extrema_surface_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/absolute_extrema_surface_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="subsec-skel-equilibrium-stability">
<title>A Physical Application: Equilibrium and Stability</title>

<p>
Consider a marble that rolls on the surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m> under the
influence of gravity. If we measure heights from the <m>xy</m>-plane,
the potential energy of the marble at the point <m>(x,y)</m> of the
surface is
<md xml:id="eq-skel-potential-energy" number="yes">
U(x,y) = mgz = mg\,f(x,y),
</md>
where <m>m</m> is the mass of the marble and <m>g</m> is the
acceleration due to gravity. The marble is in <term>equilibrium</term>
at a point where the force along the surface vanishes, which happens
exactly where both partial derivatives of the potential energy are
zero:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-equilibrium-condition" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
U_x \amp = mg\,f_x = 0,\\
U_y \amp = mg\,f_y = 0.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>

<p>
Say what <xref ref="eq-skel-equilibrium-condition"/> means
mathematically, and what the second derivative test then tells you
physically. (Since <m>mg</m> is a nonzero constant, the equilibrium
points of the marble coincide with something you have already named. Then
match each of the three conclusions of
<xref ref="thm-skel-second-derivative-test"/> to the behaviour of a
marble nudged from rest.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,7.7) {The equilibrium points are exactly:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {Local min $\to$ \qquad local max $\to$ \qquad saddle $\to$};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one identifying the equilibrium points and one matching each outcome of the second derivative test to stable or unstable equilibrium.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<example xml:id="ex-skel-marble-stability">
<title>Equilibrium and Stability of a Marble</title>
<statement>
<p>
A marble rests at the origin on each of the following three surfaces:
<md xml:id="eq-skel-three-surfaces" number="yes">
\begin{aligned}
\text{(i)} \amp \; z = x^2 + y^2,\\
\text{(ii)} \amp \; z = -x^2 - y^2,\\
\text{(iii)} \amp \; z = \frac{y^2}{3} - \frac{x^2}{3}.
\end{aligned}
</md>
</p>
<p>
Show that <m>(0,0)</m> is an equilibrium point in each case, and
determine whether the equilibrium is stable or unstable.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
(All three surfaces have appeared already in this section. For each,
check the two first partials vanish at the origin, then compute
<m>H(0,0)</m> and <m>f_{xx}(0,0)</m> and read off
<xref ref="thm-skel-second-derivative-test"/>. Translate each verdict
into what the marble does. Case (iii) is the interesting one: the marble
rolls back along one axis and away along the other <mdash/> say which, and
why that still counts as unstable.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,10.5) -- (15,10.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,13.7) {(i) $H(0,0) =$ \quad $f_{xx} =$ \quad verdict:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,10.2) {(ii) $H(0,0) =$ \quad $f_{xx} =$ \quad verdict:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {(iii) $H(0,0) =$ \quad $f_{xx} =$ \quad verdict:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {Why a saddle is unstable even though one direction restores:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, one for each of the three surfaces with its Hessian and verdict, and one for why a saddle equilibrium is unstable.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-stability-video">
<caption>
A marble at an equilibrium point on each of the three surfaces. On the
bowl <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m> a displaced marble rolls back (stable); on
the dome <m>z = -x^2 - y^2</m> it rolls away (unstable); on the saddle
<m>z = y^2/3 - x^2/3</m> it rolls back along the <m>y</m>-direction but
away along the <m>x</m>-direction (unstable).
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-skel-marble-stability"
       source="videos/stability_marble_1080p.mp4"
       preview="videos/previews/stability_marble_1080p.jpg"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-skel-stability-cross-sections">
<caption>
Cross-sections of the potential energy near an equilibrium point. Along
a direction where <m>U</m> has a minimum (blue), the force is
restoring and pushes the marble back: stable. Along a direction where
<m>U</m> has a maximum (red), the force pushes the marble away:
unstable. For the bowl, every cross-section is the blue type; for the
dome, every cross-section is the red type; a saddle has one of each.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>Two parabolas through the origin with force arrows: toward the origin on one, away from it on the other.</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Two cross-sections of the potential energy through an equilibrium point at the origin, where a green marble sits. The blue curve opens upward: the force arrows point back toward the equilibrium, so this direction is stable. The red curve opens downward: the force arrows point away from the equilibrium, so this direction is unstable.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-skel-ev-stability-cross-sections"><![CDATA[
// Two cross-sections of the potential energy U through an equilibrium point: one with
// a minimum (the force pushes the marble back) and one with a maximum (it pushes it away).
import graph;
unitsize(2.3cm);

// axes through the origin, with arrowheads and no tick marks
void axes(real xmin, real xmax, real ymin, real ymax, string xl, string yl) {
  pen axispen = black + linewidth(0.8pt);
  draw((xmin, 0)--(xmax, 0), axispen, Arrow(6));
  draw((0, ymin)--(0, ymax), axispen, Arrow(6));
  if (xl != "") label(xl, (xmax, 0), E);
  if (yl != "") label(yl, (0, ymax), N);
}

axes(-1.7, 1.7, -1.85, 1.85, "", "$U$");

real stable(real x) { return 0.9*x^2; }
real unstable(real x) { return -0.9*x^2; }
draw(graph(stable, -1.35, 1.35), blue + linewidth(1.6pt));
draw(graph(unstable, -1.35, 1.35), red + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("stable", (1.2, stable(1.2)), E, blue);
label("unstable", (1.2, unstable(1.2)), E, red);

// the force on a displaced marble: back towards the equilibrium, or away from it
draw((0.95, 0.35)--(0.5, 0.35), blue + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((-0.95, 0.35)--(-0.5, 0.35), blue + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((0.45, -0.35)--(0.9, -0.35), red + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));
draw((-0.45, -0.35)--(-0.9, -0.35), red + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow(7));

dot((0, 0), rgb(0, 0.5, 0) + linewidth(6pt));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
State the general principle these three cases illustrate.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {An equilibrium is stable exactly when:};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank box for the general principle relating stability to the potential energy.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<xi:include href="subsec-skel-fmv-why-second-derivative-test.ptx" />

</section>
